    "proof_tex": "The technique is a finite active-set stability argument applied to the compatible convex-polyhedral lift.  Fix \\(u\\in\\mathbb R^d\\).  The relative-interior tangent-lift lemma supplies \\(t_u>0\\) such that\n\\[\n q_t=p_0+tBu\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{ASC}}\n \\qquad(0<t\\le t_u). \\tag{15}\n\\]\nBy the compatible-polytope representation and fiber equivalence, \\(\\Xi(q_t)\\ne\\varnothing\\).  The feasible response weights satisfy \\(0\\le\\lambda_{xr}\\le1\\) and finitely many closed linear constraints; hence \\(\\Xi(q_t)\\) is compact.  Each separate endpoint program therefore has a primal optimizer.  To expose the dual step, write any fixed branch program as\n\\[\n \\max_x c'x\\quad\\text{subject to}\\quad Gx\\le g,\\ Hx=h. \\tag{16}\n\\]\nAt a maximizer \\(x_*\\), let \\(I\\) index its active inequalities.  There is no direction \\(d\\) satisfying \\(G_Id\\le0\\), \\(Hd=0\\), and \\(c'd>0\\), because a sufficiently small positive step from \\(x_*\\) would otherwise remain feasible and increase (16).  Applying the same finite pairwise linear-inequality elimination used above to this infeasible direction system yields multipliers \\(y_I\\ge0\\) and \\(z\\) such that\n\\[\n c=G_I'y_I+H'z. \\tag{17}\n\\]\nExtend \\(y_I\\) by zeros off \\(I\\).  Then every feasible \\(x\\) obeys\n\\[\n c'x=y'Gx+z'Hx\\le y'g+z'h,\n\\]\nwhile equality holds at \\(x_*\\) because \\(y_i(G_ix_*-g_i)=0\\).  Hence \\((y,z)\\) is a dual optimizer.  Every endpoint value is finite because the wage grid is finite.  Thus at least one primal--dual feasible optimal branch completion exists at \\(q_t\\).\n\nThere are finitely many cuts \\(\\psi(p)=\\alpha_\\psi'p+\\beta_\\psi\\).  If \\(\\psi(p_0)\\ne0\\), continuity of the affine map gives \\(t_\\psi>0\\) for which\n\\[\n \\operatorname{sign}\\{\\psi(q_t)\\}=\\operatorname{sign}\\{\\psi(p_0)\\}\n \\qquad(0<t<t_\\psi). \\tag{18}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\psi(p_0)=0\\), then exactly\n\\[\n \\psi(q_t)=t\\alpha_\\psi'Bu. \\tag{19}\n\\]\nChoose \\(t>0\\) below \\(t_u\\) and all the finitely many positive bounds in (18).  Select one feasible optimal completion at \\(q_t\\).  Equations (18)--(19) show that it agrees with every frozen base sign and with the weak tangent sign selected by \\(u\\).  If \\(\\alpha_\\psi'Bu=0\\), the equality-face convention places the cut in every incident completion.  Therefore this completion is one of the retained elements \\(b\\in\\mathcal B_0\\).  Its semantic assertion pattern has some retained representative \\(v\\in V\\), and the same weak signs state precisely that\n\\[\n u\\in P_{v,b}^0\\subseteq D_v^0. \\tag{20}\n\\]\nBecause \\(u\\) was arbitrary, (20) proves the asserted global cover.  Notice that (15), rather than feasibility of every ambient neighboring simplex law, is the only lawfulness step.",
